---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import PageHeader from '../components/PageHeader.astro';
import { SITE } from '../config';

const effective = 'November 19, 2026';
const mail = `mailto:${SITE.email}`;
---

<BaseLayout title="Terms of Use" description="The terms for using the Trust Issues iPhone app and this website.">
  <PageHeader eyebrow="Legal" title="Terms of Use" narrator="Point fingers. Officially, this time.">
    <p class="meta">Effective {effective}</p>
  </PageHeader>

  <section class="section legal-section">
    <div class="container"><div class="prose">
      <p>
        These terms apply to the Trust Issues iOS app (including TestFlight beta builds) and this website, both provided by {SITE.company}{' '}
        ({SITE.author}). By using them, you agree to these terms. If you don’t agree, please don’t use the app.
      </p>

      <h2 id="apple">Apple’s standard terms</h2>
      <p>
        If you get Trust Issues from the App Store, Apple’s{' '}
        <a href="https://www.apple.com/legal/internet-services/itunes/dev/stdeula/" rel="noopener">Licensed Application End User License Agreement</a>{' '}
        (the “Standard EULA”) applies, and these terms add to it. Beta builds distributed through TestFlight are also subject to Apple’s TestFlight
        terms. If these terms conflict with the Standard EULA, the Standard EULA wins. Apple isn’t responsible for the app or for supporting it;
        we are.
      </p>

      <h2 id="license">Your license to use the app</h2>
      <p>
        We give you a personal, non-exclusive, non-transferable, revocable license to use Trust Issues on Apple devices you own or control, for
        your own non-commercial entertainment. You may not copy, modify, reverse-engineer, resell or redistribute the app, or use it to build a
        competing service, except where the law allows it regardless of these terms. We (and our licensors) keep all rights in the app, its name,
        artwork, characters and narrator copy.
      </p>

      <h2 id="conduct">Play nice (the lying is the game, not the point)</h2>
      <p>Lying to your friends inside the game is encouraged. Outside the game, please don’t:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>harass, threaten or bully other players, or use the game to target someone;</li>
        <li>interfere with rooms you weren’t invited to, guess or scrape room codes, or try to read data you aren’t meant to see;</li>
        <li>cheat by tampering with the app, its network traffic or our servers, or try to overload or break the service;</li>
        <li>use the app in any way that breaks the law.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>We may block access or remove data that breaks these rules.</p>

      <h2 id="nicknames">Nicknames</h2>
      <p>
        You choose your own nickname, and you’re responsible for it. Nicknames are visible to everyone in your room and appear in the game log, so
        don’t use your full name, anyone else’s personal information, or anything offensive, hateful or infringing. We may remove nicknames that
        break these rules. See the <a href="/privacy/">Privacy Policy</a> for how nicknames are stored.
      </p>

      <h2 id="beta">Beta builds and changes</h2>
      <p>
        Trust Issues is under active development. Beta builds may have bugs, lose a game in progress, or change without notice. We may update,
        change or discontinue features, or the app itself, at any time. Online play depends on an internet connection and on third-party services{' '}
        (Google Firebase) that we don’t control.
      </p>

      <h2 id="warranty">No warranty</h2>
      <p>
        The app and website are provided <strong>“as is” and “as available”</strong>, without warranties of any kind, express or implied,
        including merchantability, fitness for a particular purpose and non-infringement, to the fullest extent the law allows. We don’t promise the
        app will be uninterrupted, error-free, or that your friends will ever trust you again.
      </p>

      <h2 id="liability">Limitation of liability</h2>
      <p>
        To the fullest extent permitted by law, {SITE.company} and {SITE.author} won’t be liable for any indirect, incidental, special,
        consequential or punitive damages, or any loss of data, goodwill or friendships, arising from your use of the app or website. Our total
        liability for any claim relating to them is limited to the greater of the amount you paid for the app in the 12 months before the claim,
        or US $10. Some places don’t allow these limits, so they may not apply to you.
      </p>

      <h2 id="law">Governing law</h2>
      <p>
        These terms are governed by the laws of the State of Delaware, United States, without regard to its
        conflict-of-law rules, except where the law of your country of residence requires otherwise. Disputes will be handled in the state or
        federal courts located in the State of Delaware, unless your local consumer law gives you the right to bring them
        elsewhere.
      </p>

      <h2 id="changes">Changes to these terms</h2>
      <p>
        We may update these terms from time to time. We’ll post the new version here and change the effective date. If you keep using the app
        after a change, you accept the updated terms.
      </p>

      <h2 id="contact">Contact</h2>
      <p>
        Questions about these terms? Email <a href={mail}>{SITE.email}</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .legal-section {
    padding-top: clamp(2rem, 1.5rem + 2vw, 3rem);
  }
  .meta {
    margin-top: 1rem;
    font-family: var(--font-rounded);
    font-weight: 750;
    color: var(--text-secondary);
  }
</style>
